== result (done, 55.3s)

## Spring vs. summer support reviews

Shared-themes diagram: art:89f95daee2c6

### What improved (summer review unless noted)
- **Volume handled:** the spring review reports "4,120 requests" (March–May), up from "3,380" in winter. The summer review reports "4,610 requests" (June–August).
- **First reply time:** the spring review gives a median of "3 hours 10 minutes" against a goal of "2 hours". The summer review gives "1 hour 55 minutes", inside the 2 hour goal for the first time.
- **Account access:** the spring review says requests "doubled" after the 14 April login change and plans "five articles on account access". The summer review says those five articles shipped in June, and access requests fell from "610 to 240 a month".
- **Weekend coverage:** the spring review relied on "two volunteers". The summer review says a weekend rotation replaced the volunteers and nobody worked more than two weekends.

### What did not improve
- **Staffing:** the spring review planned to "hire one more person". The summer review says the hiring plan slipped and the position is still open.
- **Billing questions:** the spring review says help articles cut repeat billing questions by "about a third". The summer review reports "380 billing questions in one week" after a July pricing change.
- **Customer satisfaction:** the summer review says it is not tracked. The spring review did not mention it.

### What stayed the same
- Both reviews cover request volume, response time, staffing, account access, weekend coverage and billing questions.
- Both reviews name next steps but no owners.
- The spring review also gave no owner or next step for the first reply time gap. That gap is now closed.

### Next steps
No owners are named in either review, so the owners below are proposals for the team lead to assign.

| Problem | Next step (source) | Owner |
|---|---|---|
| Open position | Fill it next quarter (summer review) | To be assigned, such as the support manager |
| Pricing-change spikes | Prepare billing articles before any pricing change (summer review) | To be assigned, such as the content owner |
| No satisfaction tracking | Measure customer satisfaction next quarter (summer review) | To be assigned |

Gaps in the readers' reports: the summer reader reported the weekend rotation but did not say whether it is the "shared on-call rotation" the spring review proposed. I did not read the documents directly.

